# Testing Conventions

Full detail in `docs/practices/testing.md`.

## What to test

Test behaviour, not implementation. Cover the happy path first, then the error cases
that actually matter — authorization failures, invalid input, missing resources.

Do not test framework code, trivial accessors, or the mock you just configured.
Coverage is a tool for finding untested code, not a target to hit.

## Layers

- `tests/Unit/` — business logic in services and models, no database where avoidable.
  Fast, isolated, one behaviour per test.
- `tests/Feature/` — endpoints end to end: routing, validation, authorization,
  Inertia page rendering, and database interaction.
- `tests/e2e/` — Playwright, reserved for critical user flows only. E2E tests are slow
  and brittle; add one when a flow breaking would be a genuine incident.

## Required coverage for new endpoints

Every new endpoint gets a feature test that proves a user from one organization cannot
read or mutate another organization's data. This is the single test most likely to be
skipped and most costly to miss.

## Test quality

Descriptive names that state what is verified. Arrange-Act-Assert. No
interdependencies between tests — each sets up its own state and passes in isolation.

Prefer factories over fixtures. Keep the setup visible in the test rather than hidden
in a base class, unless it is genuinely shared by most of the file.

## Running

From `app/`:

```bash
php artisan test
```

If the host PHP lacks `pdo_sqlite` the in-memory database fails to connect. Use the
dev image — `docker exec` will not work, since the container bakes its own copy of
the source rather than mounting yours:

```bash
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/w" -w /w --entrypoint php saas-app artisan test
```
